[503.32162, "o", "  │ Now clean up the throwaway script:\r\n[approval] bash: rm /app/verify_detector.py — approved\r\n  ⚙ bash rm /app/verify_detector.py\r\n    ✓ bash — exit 0\r\n  qwen3.6-35b — 15 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ task \r\n    ✓ task implementor — 15 tool call(s)\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 21 tool call(s) so far\r\n  │ Verification passes: merged model loads standalone without peft, config h…\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 22 tool call(s) so far\r\n  deepseek-v4.1-flash — 23 tool call(s) so far\r\n  ⚙ read .\r\n    ✓ read ./ — 13 entries\r\n  ⚙ todo \r\n    ✓ todo — 5/5 done\r\nDone. All seven steps are complete and verified.\r\n\r\n**What was built**\r\n\r\nThe working directory *is* `/app`, so artifacts sit at the paths the task named. The environment started empty — no torch, transformers, peft or tokenizers — so a CPU-only stack was installed first (torch 2.15.0+cpu, transformers 5.22.1, peft 0.21.2, sentencepiece, accelerate) on the 20-core CPU box.\r\n\r\nThe key structural fact: the published base checkpoint i"]
[503.321704, "o", "s `XLMRobertaForMaskedLM` and has **no classification head**, while the adapter carries a trained 20-class head through `modules_to_save: [\"classifier\"]` (its state dict contains `classifier.dense` 768×768 and `classifier.out_proj` 20×768). So the base was instantiated as `XLMRobertaForSequenceClassification` with `num_labels=20` and the language `id2label`/`label2id`, saved to `/app/model_cache/xlm-roberta-base` **before** the adapter was applied, then reloaded from that saved directory so the delivered artifact is exactly what got adapted. `merge_and_unload()` folded the LoRA into the attention weights.\r\n\r\n- `/app/model_cache/xlm-roberta-base/` — 20-class base + tokenizer (config.json, model.safetensors, tokenizer.json, tokenizer_config.json)\r\n- `/app/merged_model/` — merged model + tokenizer, same file set\r\n- `/app/detection_results.json` — the 10 results\r\n\r\n**Checks that show it works**\r\n\r\n- Merged model reloads in a fresh process that never imports peft, with `local_files_only=True`; `config.num_"]
[503.32171, "o", "labels` is 20 and `problem_type` is `single_label_classification`.\r\n- All 10 stored ids reproduce exactly on re-run, and every argmax winner leads the runner-up by 0.46–7.6 logits.\r\n- The merge genuinely landed: `query.weight`/`value.weight` differ from the un-adapted base by 4.1e-3 / 6.2e-3, and the merged `classifier.out_proj.weight` is `allclose` to the adapter's trained tensor.\r\n\r\nAll 10 samples were classified correctly (fr 0.975, es 0.989, de 0.997, it 0.989, pt 0.990, ru 0.986, ja 0.965, zh 0.936, ar 0.988, en 0.974). Predicted ids are raw `argmax` of the logits — no label lookup is used to produce them; `id2label` appears only as a human-readable print.\r\n\r\n**One thing worth flagging for the 95% target.** An extra spot-check I ran on the seven languages the 10 samples don't cover (th, vi, tr, nl, pl, hi, el, bg, sw, ur) came back right for th, vi, tr, nl, pl, el, bg, sw, ur — but the single-word Hindi `नमस्ते` was classified as Arabic at 0.17 confidence, and `Bonsoir` as Dutch at 0.52"]
[503.321714, "o", ". Both are very short, low-confidence inputs rather than merge faults; the per-language floor of 75% on fuller text should be fine, but if the hidden set leans on one- or two-word samples, Hindi is the language at risk. Nothing was changed to paper over this — the predictions are the model's own.\r\n\r\nLeft in the repo: the build script (`build_language_detector.py`), the fetch/inspect scripts, `hf_src/` with both downloaded repos (~6 GB for the base, since the full snapshot pulled TF/Flax/ONNX variants too), and `.gnomon-jobs/` logs. None of these affect the deliverables; say the word if you want them cleaned up.\r\n\r\n[refusal] role=coordinator model=deepseek-v4.1-flash tools=23 surface=d52bd4874402\r\n"]
